Log file closed\line\line\f0 OK, that's the first pass at it.\line\line Now to get some of the details.\par
\par
For a typical line, the first word is the forward pointer to the address of the next line.  The last forward pointer contains 0000 on the last line.  Junk typically follows and should always be ignored.\par
\par
The second word is the line number word.\par
\par
Note:  All FOCAL line numbers are stored as follows:\line\line Bits[0-4] group number.\line\line Bits[5-11] the local line number in the group.\par
\par
The text buffer starts at 0200 [in whatever field this was created in].  This is apparent because the pointer words are consistent with this.  Moreover, it just makes good sense to reserve page zero of any memory field.\line\line Case-code ASCII is used.  77 is the reserved case flip, thus ? and the _ character are not allowed.  Back then, the latter was the \cf1\highlight2\fs29\u8592?\cf0\highlight0\fs20  character.\line\line All lines must be terminated with the expansion of <CR> which is 77 15.  If there is an extra halfword, it is filled with 77 [which is ignored].  When flipped, you get lower-case printable.\page Line breakdowns (Note: researched backwards from the end because it was easier for me that way, and some things realized along the way.)\par
\par
\f1 1:0016.000 0476 5051 0224 0000 0340 2557 2755 0617 *D>()BT@@C U/W-FO*\par
1:0016.010 0301 1472 0000 2315 2317 2224 0000 6165 *CAL:@@SMSORT@@15*\par
1:0016.020 5661 6056 6770 7715 \f0                    \f1  *.10.78?M\f0         \f1 *\par
\par
\f0 I don't know the significance of the 0476 at relative 000 of the file.  Somewhere I read about the 5051 being a U/W FOCAL-ism, some internal signature I guess because the program evolved and it has to do with capabilities as what they did to it parts company with the standard FOCAL language, which obviously happened (and not necessarily welcome).  This is right out of the MIT playbook where students were never happy with standards, so they just broke them, thus the "TECO of the week" so show an analogous situation.  Some of it is clearly chipping away at the language by allowing quirky syntax "JUST BECAUSE" but some other things are making quirky variants in the language (which really works well when you want to teach something and no two documents are alike because they cannot agree in what the subject matter is).\line\line The rest is standard FOCAL innards.   The first word is the forward pointer 0224 since the text buffer is somehow implied as going into x0200 where x is the field they used.  Totally made up happenstance, so now they are stuck with it working that way.  I see everything about this project as sloppy and memory wasteful in a time when it was a BIG DEAL to want another 4K.  Seems they don't understand the notion of get something back for having paid for the hardware.  This is a rather slim justification for all that memory usage considering the then-current cost.  Overall, I am underwhelmed.\line\line You have to read what follows in reverse order, so this is really my conclusion.  But yes, you can do some minimal validation, and if it flunks, then who knows, or if half-assed validation works, perhaps one of the other programs also thinks of itself as *the* definitive FOCAL.  That can be part of your program later, etc.\line\line This can be displayed as:\par
\par
C U/W-FOCAL: @@SMSORT@@15.10.78\line\line There is no line number which is a standard part of the "badge" or "emblem" part of the buffer that is permanent before typing in even the first line.  All FOCAL programs do that.   Apparently some internal quirkiness to allow an internal functional title based on the non-extension filename.  And then the date text.  Notice DD/MM/YY which is very European and unexpected and arbitrary considering where this was written (perhaps just an arrogant conceit).  Perhaps there is a way to assemble the program for alternate date format.  Certainly not welcome in OS/8 which never handles dates that way, etc. If you want to bother, clearly the 0000 words are flags that the next that follows is the file name (no .FC; it apparently is required thus implicit in their little world) and then the text of the date of the file, thus you could turn it around with impunity into mm/dd/yy and because this is OS/8 you could add the leading 19xx on the year; it wouldn't be wrong.\line\line I wrote P?S/8 date handling inherently Y2K-proof from the beginning.  There is a secondary issue few have dealt with: If you allow two-digit years (which the P?S/8 DATE command does) you have to define the "favored century" the two-digit form applies to.  Today, perhaps people want 2010 forward to 2050 and maybe back from 1980 to 1999.  Thus, the code, written in the 1970s was actually thinking quite ahead.\line\line In any case, in P?S/8, either will do for now:\line\line .DATE 18-SEP-2017\par
or\par
.DATE 18-SEP-17\page Line breakdowns with FOCAL line numbers.\f1\par
